// ==========================================================================
// data/planes/cessna172/flow5.jsonc for the Cessna 172 source analysis, generated -- do not hand-edit.
//
// 1. SOLVER, MESH, FLIGHT CONDITION
//    Solver: flow5, via aid.flow5_io.write_flow5_deck / run_flow5(ac, mesh) and
//    aid.flow5_controls.flow5_controls, from the sibling package imported from ../USAF_DATCOM/AircraftIntuitiveDesign/Python/src,
//    a path relative to this repository's root so the bytes do not depend on which
//    mount point the repo is reached through; see provenance.aid_src.
//    Sibling commit: e0d2b992003fe7c81682dabcf31777f187961228.  It is recorded
//    because an upstream commit has broken this converter twice, silently both times -- a rebuild
//    that moves it should be a one-line diff in this field and nothing else.
//    Mesh: 10 spanwise x 10 chordwise, read by write_flow5_deck as (ny, nx).  The deck
//    carries WG, HT, VT and NP[0] ("Wing 2"), so NP[0] IS in this file even though it is
//    not in avl.jsonc (AVL keeps at most three surfaces).
//    The runner emits 26 keys and this file fills 21 of the 25 slots from them:
//      CD, CDind, CDvis, CL, CLa, CXa, CY, CYb, CYp, CYr, CZa, Cl, Clb, Clp, Clr, Cm, Cma, Cn, Cnb, Cnp, Cnr, Cx, Cz, XNP, alpha, beta
//    The frame is settled in ONE call, aid.flow5_io.run_flow5, which is
//    to_frd('flow5', run_flow5_native(...)).  Hand-applying the sign map would be
//    wrong in a way that is worth stating: flow5's per-point polar channels
//    Cx/Cz/Cl/Cn ARE in its sign map with a -1 (raw Cz tracks raw CL, i.e. it is
//    up-positive and is NOT a body-z coefficient), while its twelve
//    StabDerivatives take NO entry because computeStabilityDerivatives projects
//    them onto the stability axes.  Measured: CZa = -5.256228 while CLa = +5.169735,
//    so CZa already carries this slot's sign.  Negating the derivative scalars would
//    invert the lift slope.
//    aid/flow5_controls' DEFAULT runner is the RAW one, so to_frd is injected as
//    the runner here; without it the aileron Cl and Cn arrive with the wrong sign.
//    Evaluated at alpha = 4 deg, the middle entry of AERO.ALSCHD, as aid/tornado_io.py::_build_state picks it.
//    Flight condition: the deck is ENTIRELY SI -- sref_m2, cref_m, bref_m, cog_m and qinf_mps -- so
//    qinf = 10.2073 m/s is the PHYSICAL speed and there is no unit slip on this path at
//    all, unlike the 10.2073 AID labels ft/s in tornado.jsonc and avl.jsonc.
//    Mach 0.03, altitude 0.0 ft, rho = 1.225056 kg/m^3.  The trimmed cruise speed the model
//    actually flies at is 23.1114 m/s, a factor 2.264 higher, so this file's CLa
//    and Cma describe a slower and therefore thinner wing than the one flown.
//    That factor is 2.26 here and 7.43 in tornado.jsonc and
//    avl.jsonc for one reason only: flow5's deck is SI, so its 10.2073 really is 10.2073 m/s,
//    while the other two feed 10.2073 into a FEET frame, where it means 3.1112 m/s.
//    The rate factor is 1 for three of the four rate slots this file does have,
//    because flow5's StabDerivatives are per unit of p-hat / r-hat taken at the
//    source speed and are NOT rescaled -- the same treatment as the other three
//    files, for the same reason, and recorded the same way.
//
// 2. THE SOURCE PLANFORM IS A CESSNA 172 AT ROUGHLY 1/3 LINEAR SCALE
//    span 12 ft against a real 36.1 ft, chord 2 ft against 4.89 ft, area 24 ft^2
//    against 174 ft^2.  Wing loading is therefore 6.70 lbf/ft^2 where a real C172 is
//    13.2.  Nothing in this file may be compared with a full-scale figure.
//
// 3. MASS
//    mass_kg = 72.574 is derived from the source's own AERO.WT = 5 slugs:
//    floor(AERO.WT * 32.0 ft/s^2 / 2.2046226 lbf/kg * 1000)/1000 = 72.574 kg, truncated from
//    72.5747799 kg, i.e. 160 lbf.  A slug is a mass unit, so the
//    source's WT converts instead as AERO.WT * SLUG_TO_KG = 72.96951 kg; the plan's figure assumes g = 32.0 ft/s^2, which
//    appears nowhere in the source (its own G_FT_S2 is 32.174), so the plan is 0.54 % low and
//    everything derived from the weight is too.
//    This is NOT a geometrically similar 1/3 scale C172, which would weigh about 53.5 kg, so no
//    scaling of a published full-scale number is self-consistent and none was attempted.
//    ixx, iyy, izz come from radius-of-gyration rules on THIS mass and THIS span (see below);
//    ixz = 0 and he = 0.  All four Cessna files derive mass and inertia identically from the same
//    geometry, so they are consistent with each other and with tornado.jsonc.
//    xcg and xcg_ref are BOTH 0.0 here, where tornado.jsonc carries -0.896112 for both, and that
//    difference is deliberate rather than an oversight: these three solvers report about the CG
//    itself (verified in item 5), so the CG's offset FROM THE MOMENT REFERENCE is zero, while for
//    Tornado it is the 2.94 ft station shift.  The schema's own `Cm + Cz*(xcgref - xcg)` term is
//    identically zero either way, because xcg == xcg_ref on every reachable path.
//
// 4. UNIT CONVERSIONS APPLIED, AND THE ONES NOT TRUSTED
//    length: ft x 0.3048 = 0.3048 m, applied by aid.flow5_units inside write_flow5_deck; angle:
//    degrees x 57.29577951308232 (= DEG_TO_RAD) per radian.
//    NOTHING HAD TO BE REPAIRED on this path.  flow5's deck and its polar are
//    SI end to end and its CLa and Cma are already PER RADIAN (flow5_run.cpp's
//    `ols_slope_rad` regresses CL and Cm on alpha in radians).  The only
//    per-degree numbers here are aid.flow5_controls' central differences, which
//    it forms at delta +- aid.control_deriv.H_DEG = +-1 DEGREE, so each control
//    slot is the solver's own number x 57.29577951308232.
//    Every array in this file is per radian and every length is SI.  numpy bridge: numpy.trapezoid present; no alias bound
//    cx[0] -- the parasite drag slot, Cd0 in a drag build-up -- = 0.017219.
//    Source: the source analysis file's own DATCOM-computed WG.CD0, the same value tornado.jsonc, datcom.jsonc and avl.jsonc all use, and it is NOT a flow5 output. flow5 now SPLITS
//    its drag and the split is the measurement that settles this slot:
//      CD(0)    = 0.00124344
//      CDvis(0) = 0   <- identically 0.0 on EVERY alpha
//      CDind(0) = 0.00124344   <- identically CD on every alpha
//    because FLOW5/run/flow5_run.cpp calls `pPlPolar->setViscous(false)`: the deck
//    asks for no viscous model, so flow5 reports ZERO profile drag and the whole of its CD
//    is induced.  Neither channel is informative about parasite drag, so cx[0] is not taken from
//    either.  The source file's own DATCOM-computed WG.CD0 is used instead, exactly as it already is
//    for tornado.jsonc, so all four files agree on it.
//
// 5. THE MOMENT REFERENCE SHIFT
//    NO MOMENT SHIFT WAS APPLIED, and that is verified rather than assumed.  write_flow5_deck puts
//    the source's own AERO.XCG into the deck's `cog_m` as [0.896112, 0, 0] m, which is
//    ft(2.94) = 0.896112 m, and this converter
//    read it back out of the deck it built and compared it with AERO.XCG.  A mismatch raises.
//    dx = dy = dz = 0, so cm[1], cm[0] and cm[2] are used as they stand.  Contrast
//    tornado.jsonc, whose ref_point is 2.94 ft AHEAD of the CG and is corrected by dx = -0.896112 m.
//
// 6. SIGN CONVENTION
//    cz is the body +z force coefficient with z DOWN, so cz = -CL and cz[1] is NEGATIVE for a
//    stable aircraft (CL_alpha = -cz[1] > 0), and cm[1] is negative too.  That identity is
//    load-bearing; it is what makes data/planes/linear/morelli.json's cz[1] = -4.5 CORRECT, not a
//    defect of that file.
//    data/planes/linear/morelli.json is the sign-convention authority wherever it disagrees with
//    data/planes/f16/morelli.json.  It WINS on cm[1] (-0.8 against +0.0466) and on cnp[0] (-0.03
//    against +0.0268), where a positive value would be statically unstable.  It LOSES on cnda[0]
//    (+0.01 against -0.0335), where adverse yaw fixes the sign.  A reader must not assume the two
//    sets share a convention: this file and linear/morelli.json agree on cz[1] and disagree with
//    the F-16 set on cm[1] and cnp[0].
//    flow5 reports about its `cog_m` in the SAME x-forward, y-right, z-DOWN frame
//    plane/dynamics.py uses: its CL is up-positive (CLa > 0), so the body-z force
//    coefficient is its negative, and its Cm is nose-up positive.  So NO axis mapping and NO
//    moment shift is applied.  Which slots that leaves negated is DERIVED from
//    provenance.mapping rather than asserted -- this item used to claim
//    `cz[1] = -CL_alpha`, which was true while cz[1] came from CLa and stopped
//    being true when the source became the body-axis CZa, which needs no
//    negation at all:
//    1 negation survives, and none is a frame conversion.  Each is a SIGN-CONVENTION identity that the schema
//    itself requires -- `cz = -CL` for the lift slots, its body +z axis pointing down, and
//    `cx = -CD` for the drag slot -- not a change of axis.  Listed from provenance.mapping:
//      cz[5] from aid.flow5_controls[elevator].CL = 0.010631 per degree -> -0.609126 per radian
//    Its Cma = -1.573326 is already negative, the
//    invariant sign, so it is written through unnegated.
//    Every sign is decided mechanically by aero_convert.units.normalise_sign against SIGN_INVARIANTS.
//    cm0 (Cm0) carries NO invariant -- the two in-tree reference files disagree on it and its
//    sign is tail rigging, not a derivable convention -- so it is written through untouched and is
//    recorded as `not-normalised` in provenance.not_normalised.  Three provenance states exist and
//    all three are recorded: `missing` (field None, slot 0.0), `flipped` (sign normalised against
//    its invariant row) and `not-normalised` (cm0).
//
//    A CAVEAT on the NINE genuinely per-phat / per-qhat / per-rhat slots:
//    cyp[0], cyr[0], czq[0], cmq[0], clp[0], clr[0], cnp[0], cnr[0], cxq[0].
//      NOT rescaled, as in tornado.jsonc. This solver quotes every rate
//      derivative per p*b/(2V), q*cbar/(2V) or r*b/(2V) at the SOURCE
//      Mach-0.03 speed V = 10.2073 m/s, while f16/aero_morelli.py forms phat
//      = p*b_m/(2*V) at the trimmed cruise V = 23.1114 m/s. Restoring the
//      solver's physical dC/dq would mean multiplying the nine per-hat slots
//      by V_trim/V_source = 2.26419, because czq[0]*cbar/(2*V_trim) must
//      equal CL_q*cbar/(2*V_source); the model's rate-damping terms are that
//      much weaker than the solver implies. Note the plan writes that factor
//      as AS_source/V_trim, which is the INVERSE of the direction that
//      restores dC/dq; nothing is rescaled here either way, so the question
//      is only ever a note.
//    cy[1] and cy[2] are NOT in that list: they are cy_da and cy_dr, the aileron
//    and rudder side force per radian of CONTROL DEFLECTION, which no
//    flight-condition speed touches.
//
//    4 OF THE 25 SLOTS ARE ABSENT -- zeroed and declared
//    in provenance.missing with a reason rather than guessed.  Both lists below are
//    GENERATED from the run's own coverage block, so they cannot go stale against
//    the slot table:
//      produced (21): cd0, cl0, cl_alpha, cl_beta, cl_da, cl_de, cl_dr, cl_p, cl_r, cm0, cm_alpha, cm_de, cn_beta, cn_da, cn_dr, cn_p, cn_r, cy_beta, cy_dr, cy_p, cy_r
//      absent   (4): cd_q, cl_q, cm_q, cy_da
//      cmq[0]    cm_q    ZEROED AND DECLARED -- no value exists: pitch-rate derivative of Cm: flow5's library DOES compute CXq / CZq / Cmq, but FLOW5/run/flow5_run.cpp's stab_derivative_fields() does not forward them, so they never reach the JSON.  No q-derivative of any kind is available
//      cxq[0]    cd_q    ZEROED AND DECLARED -- no value exists: pitch-rate derivative of the axial force: flow5's library DOES compute CXq / CZq / Cmq, but FLOW5/run/flow5_run.cpp's stab_derivative_fields() does not forward them, so they never reach the JSON.  No q-derivative of any kind is available
//      cy[1]     cy_da   ZEROED AND DECLARED -- no value exists: aileron side force: aid/flow5_controls' _KEEP table asks the aileron for Cl and Cn only, and does not ask for CY at all, so no aileron side-force coefficient is computed
//      czq[0]    cl_q    ZEROED AND DECLARED -- no value exists: pitch-rate derivative of roll: flow5's library DOES compute CXq / CZq / Cmq, but FLOW5/run/flow5_run.cpp's stab_derivative_fields() does not forward them, so they never reach the JSON.  No q-derivative of any kind is available

//    SEPARATELY, 4 SLOT(S) ARE FILLED BUT FLIPPED -- these are NOT absent:
//    they carry a real solver value whose sign the invariant table reversed, so
//    the solver's own sign sits on the other side of zero: cl_da, cl_dr, cn_dr, cn_p
//    Every one is in the slot table above with state `flipped`.
//    This is flow5's real capability, measured, not a shortfall of this adapter:
//    its runner emits 26 keys over the alpha sweep (CD, CDind, CDvis, CL, CLa, CXa, CY, CYb, CYp, CYr, CZa, Cl, Clb, Clp, Clr, Cm, Cma, Cn, Cnb, Cnp, Cnr, Cx, Cz, XNP, alpha, beta),
//    and of those only CL, CD, Cm, CLa and Cma are longitudinal coefficients the
//    schema has a slot for.  flow5 is a LONGITUDINAL cross-check on the lift
//    slope, the pitching slope, the two intercepts, the elevator and the parasite
//    drag -- it is not a 6-DOF data set and must not be flown as one.
//    CD_alpha is not representable in this schema (cx has no CD*alpha term) and is 0.0.
//    cd_de is not a FIELD at all: flow5 does produce an elevator drag derivative, but this
//    schema has no slot for it in any of the 19 arrays, so it maps to nothing.  It is NOT
//    carried in this file; the note at the end of item 7 says where the build put it.
//    One magnitude sits outside the plan's band and is RECORDED rather than forced: cm[1] = -1.573326
//    /rad is 4.9 % below the plan's -1.5 ... -0.3 pitched-moment-slope band, which that band was
//    estimated from (tornado.jsonc ships -1.1927).  The sign and the static margin are both
//    correct and the band is the outlier here, not the coefficient.  Task 5's
//    invariant suite is where magnitudes are asserted, so this excursion is
//    declared here rather than forced into the band.
//
// 7. PER-SLOT PROVENANCE (all 19 arrays; real solver output vs zeroed)
//   cz[1]     cl_alpha   normalised     solver flow5 CZa (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = -5.25623 -> mapped -5.25623 -> written -5.25623
//   cm[1]     cm_alpha   normalised     solver flow5 Cma = -1.57333 -> mapped -1.57333 -> written -1.57333
//   cz[0]     cl0        normalised     solver flow5 Cz at alpha = 0 deg = -0.131536 -> mapped -0.131536 -> written -0.131536
//   cm[0]     cm0        not-normalised solver flow5 Cm at alpha = 0 deg = 0.000273421 -> mapped 0.000273421 -> written 0.000273421
//   cx[0]     cd0        normalised     NOT solver output -- cd0 <- source WG.CD0 (NOT a flow5 output) -> mapped -0.017219 -> written -0.017219
//   cl[0]     cl_beta    normalised     solver flow5 Clb (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = -0.0530087 -> mapped -0.0530087 -> written -0.0530087
//   cy[0]     cy_beta    normalised     solver flow5 CYb (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = -0.410706 -> mapped -0.410706 -> written -0.410706
//   cn[0]     cn_beta    normalised     solver flow5 Cnb (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = 0.168534 -> mapped 0.168534 -> written 0.168534
//   cyp[0]    cy_p       normalised     solver flow5 CYp (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = -0.207291 -> mapped -0.207291 -> written -0.207291
//   cyr[0]    cy_r       normalised     solver flow5 CYr (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = 0.410264 -> mapped 0.410264 -> written 0.410264
//   clp[0]    cl_p       normalised     solver flow5 Clp (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = -0.53849 -> mapped -0.53849 -> written -0.53849
//   clr[0]    cl_r       normalised     solver flow5 Clr (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = 0.0267396 -> mapped 0.0267396 -> written 0.0267396
//   cnp[0]    cn_p       flipped        solver flow5 Cnp (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = 0.0403024 -> mapped 0.0403024 -> written -0.0403024
//   cnr[0]    cn_r       normalised     solver flow5 Cnr (StabDerivative, already stability-axis) = -0.206016 -> mapped -0.206016 -> written -0.206016
//   clda[0]   cl_da      flipped        solver aid.flow5_controls[aileron].Cl = 0.00559177 -> mapped 0.320385 -> written -0.320385
//   cnda[0]   cn_da      normalised     solver aid.flow5_controls[aileron].Cn = -0.000603929 -> mapped -0.0346026 -> written -0.0346026
//   cz[5]     cl_de      normalised     solver aid.flow5_controls[elevator].CL = 0.0106313 -> mapped -0.609126 -> written -0.609126
//   cm[2]     cm_de      normalised     solver aid.flow5_controls[elevator].Cm = -0.0320606 -> mapped -1.83694 -> written -1.83694
//   cldr[0]   cl_dr      flipped        solver aid.flow5_controls[rudder].Cl = -0.000557344 -> mapped -0.0319334 -> written 0.0319334
//   cy[2]     cy_dr      normalised     solver aid.flow5_controls[rudder].CY = 0.00508355 -> mapped 0.291266 -> written 0.291266
//   cndr[0]   cn_dr      flipped        solver aid.flow5_controls[rudder].Cn = 0.00275685 -> mapped 0.157956 -> written -0.157956
//   czq[0]    cl_q       missing        ABSENT -- flow5 polar / aid.flow5_controls (see provenance.missing) -> mapped n/a -> written 0.0 (missing)
//   cmq[0]    cm_q       missing        ABSENT -- flow5 polar / aid.flow5_controls (see provenance.missing) -> mapped n/a -> written 0.0 (missing)
//   cxq[0]    cd_q       missing        ABSENT -- flow5 polar / aid.flow5_controls (see provenance.missing) -> mapped n/a -> written 0.0 (missing)
//   cy[1]     cy_da      missing        ABSENT -- flow5 polar / aid.flow5_controls (see provenance.missing) -> mapped n/a -> written 0.0 (missing)
//   every slot in plane.groups.nonlinear_index is exactly 0.0; CD_alpha is not
//   representable in this schema (cx has no CD*alpha term) and is zeroed and declared
//    THE REST OF THIS SOLVER'S RAW, UNCONVERTED OUTPUT IS IN geometry.jsonc, under raw_cross_checks.flow5
//    -- the Fortran's own for006 table and AID's pre-correction handbook rows for
//    DATCOM, the per-alpha .st rows for AVL, the deck and the polar for flow5.
//    A model file's provenance.mapping carries only the one raw number behind each
//    slot, so that block is what makes the rest re-derivable without re-running.
//
// 8. HOW TO REGENERATE THIS FILE
//    cd python && python3 scripts/build_cessna172_aero.py
//    Nothing else writes these files; the sibling analysis and the sibling aid tree
//    are read-only inputs and all solver scratch goes to a temporary directory.
// ==========================================================================
