      "why_pinned": "flow5's Clb is MESH-DEPENDENT and the sibling records it: -0.0736 at ('5','3'), +0.0018 at ('10','5'), -0.0530 at ('10','10'), -0.0593 at ('20','10'), against AVL's -0.059 and Tornado's -0.054.  At ('10','5') it goes POSITIVE and |Clb| is ~30x too small, which reads as a sign flip rather than a convergence failure.  ('10','10') is the sibling's own default and the only one of the four that agrees with AVL, so it is pinned by test_16_flow5_mesh_is_pinned because the hazard is a silent sign inversion, not a precision loss"
    },
    "source": "../USAF_DATCOM/AircraftIntuitiveDesign/Analyses/Cessna172.jsonc",
    "aid_src": "../USAF_DATCOM/AircraftIntuitiveDesign/Python/src",
    "aid_src_from_env": false,
    "aid_src_commit": "e0d2b992003fe7c81682dabcf31777f187961228",
    "flight_condition": {
      "mach": 0.03,
      "alt_ft": 0.0,
      "alpha_deg": 4.0,
      "alpha_schedule_deg": null,
      "alpha_index_rule": "the middle entry of AERO.ALSCHD, as aid/tornado_io.py::_build_state picks it",
      "betha_rad": 0.0,
      "rho_kg_m3": 1.2250558830000002,
      "v_source_mps": 10.20734548754484,
      "v_trim_mps": 23.11135677910822,
      "speed_ratio_v_trim_over_v_source": 2.2641887459681906,
      "a_sound_ftps": 1116.288876590643,
      "v_true_mps": 10.20734548754484,
      "speed_unit_note": "AID's drivers hand over `mach * atm['a'] / 3.28084` = 10.207345 and label it ft/s (Tornado, AVL), which is why v_source_mps is that number x 0.3048.  The same expression in SI -- which is what flow5's deck actually used -- is the physical speed 10.2073 m/s at a sound speed of 1116.2889 ft/s.  Both readings are recorded and neither is reconciled away",
      "rate_derivative_note": "NOT rescaled, as in tornado.jsonc.  This solver quotes every rate derivative per p*b/(2V), q*cbar/(2V) or r*b/(2V) at the SOURCE Mach-0.03 speed V = 10.2073 m/s, while f16/aero_morelli.py forms phat = p*b_m/(2*V) at the trimmed cruise V = 23.1114 m/s.  Restoring the solver's physical dC/dq would mean multiplying the nine per-hat slots by V_trim/V_source = 2.26419, because czq[0]*cbar/(2*V_trim) must equal CL_q*cbar/(2*V_source); the model's rate-damping terms are that much weaker than the solver implies.  Note the plan writes that factor as AS_source/V_trim, which is the INVERSE of the direction that restores dC/dq; nothing is rescaled here either way, so the question is only ever a note."
    },
    "moment_reference": {
      "solver_station_ft": 2.939999905920003,
      "aero_xcg_ft": 2.94,
      "tolerance_ft": 0.0001,
      "dx_m": 0.0,
      "dy_m": 0.0,
      "dz_m": 0.0,
      "note": "flow5 reports every moment about AERO.XCG, and the station was read back out of the input the SOLVER was given rather than out of the same in-memory value, then compared with the source; dx = dy = dz = 0, so no moment shift is applied"
    },
    "sign_convention": {
      "solver_axes": "flow5 after to_frd: its per-point polar channels Cx/Cz/Cl/Cn take a -1 and its twelve StabDerivatives take none, because they are already stability-axis -- so the adapter contains no frame mapping at all and no moment shift",
      "dynamics_axes": "x forward, y right, z down (plane/dynamics.py)",
      "cz": "cz = -CL (body z down); this is the load-bearing identity and it is why cz[1] is negative for a stable aircraft",
      "authority": "data/planes/linear/morelli.json is the sign-convention authority where it disagrees with data/planes/f16/morelli.json: it wins cm[1] (-0.8 against +0.0466) and cnp[0] (-0.03 against +0.0268) and loses cnda[0] (+0.01 against -0.0335).  Its cz[1] = -4.5 AGREES with cz = -CL and is correct, not a defect",
      "normalisation": "aero_convert.units.normalise_sign against SIGN_INVARIANTS; no sign is applied by hand anywhere in this adapter",
      "not_normalised": "cm0 (Cm0) carries no invariant -- the two in-tree reference files disagree on it and its sign is tail rigging -- so it is written through and recorded in provenance.not_normalised"
    },
    "unit_conversions": {
      "length": "ft x 0.3048",
      "per_degree_to_per_radian": 57.29577951308232,
      "aid_helpers_not_trusted": "aid.stability._per_deg, aid.handbook_controls._per_deg and aid.handbook_pass._per_rad are all `if a > 1: convert else pass`; every per-degree number here is multiplied by DEG_TO_RAD explicitly instead",
      "mass": "the source's own AERO.WT, see mass"
    },
    "mass": {
      "source": "AERO.WT = 5 slugs",
      "mass_kg": 72.574,
      "derivation": "floor(AERO.WT * 32.0 ft/s^2 / 2.2046226 lbf/kg * 1000) / 1000 = 72.574",
      "exact_kg_truncated_to_3dp": 72.57477991924786,
      "weight_lbf": 160.0,
      "slug_conversion_kg": 72.96951469999999,
      "standard_gravity_used_ft_s2": 32.0,
      "standard_gravity_in_source_ft_s2": 32.174,
      "note": "A slug is a mass unit, so the source's own WT converts as AERO.WT * SLUG_TO_KG = 72.96951 kg.  The plan instead states 72.574 kg, which is AERO.WT * 32.0 ft/s^2 over 2.2046226 lbf/kg, i.e. it assumes a standard gravity of 32.0 ft/s^2.  That figure appears nowhere in the source (G_FT_S2 = 32.174 here), so the plan is 0.54 % low.  The plan's number ships because its approved design fixes it and plan test 6 pins it verbatim in the header; weight, trim qbar, V and t_max_n are therefore all that much low.  All four Cessna files derive it the same way, so they are consistent with each other"
    },
